# Blue Shift HD crowbar. Measured by vrcardgen, finished by hand.
#
# Part J: "A physical object with mass. Hits are found by sweeping the
# crowbar's head (declared in the card) between consecutive commands against
# hitboxes and the world, on the server; damage scales with the head's speed
# at contact, with a floor below which nothing happens. Wedge the tip into a
# breakable vent, grate or crate and lever it: sustained torque applies
# damage until it gives."
#
# This is the weapon vr_strike.cpp was written for, so the card is the whole
# mechanism: no type, because nothing feeds it, and no joints, because
# nothing on it moves relative to itself. What it has is a head and a tip,
# and this card is where they get declared.
#
# WHICH MODEL. bshift_hd's crowbar is its own rig and not a copy of anything:
# 12 bones where valve has 11, bshift 26, and valve_hd and gearbox_hd 47
# apiece. All six retail crowbars hash differently, so this card binds to
# bshift_hd and declines the rest rather than driving the wrong geometry.
# The rig is one weapon bone - `Bip01 R Hand`, carrying all 256 crowbar
# vertices - plus an arm and a sleeve on `R_Arm_bone`. There is nothing to
# pose, which is why the generator found 0 drivable joints and was right.
#
# THE COORDINATES BELOW ARE THAT BONE'S SPACE, which for a one-bone weapon is
# the weapon frame. The crowbar runs along +z from a butt at z -6.10 to the
# bend at z 20.18, a 1.28-square bar through the grip, and the claw turns out
# in +x to x 8.27. 27.17 units corner to corner.

weapon v_crowbar
match  bones 12  seqs 11  hash 0x09482809

# THE HEAD IS THE SMALLEST BALL THAT HOLDS IT. Separating the crowbar from
# the hand by texture (`crowbar_01.bmp`, 256 verts; the hand and sleeve are
# their own meshes) leaves a far-end mass at z >= 17.5 that is the bend, the
# claw and the wedge together: 192 vertices, x 2.56..8.27, y 0.72..2.87,
# z 17.94..20.18. Its minimal enclosing sphere is centred here with a radius
# of 2.894, and that ball contains all 192 and exactly none of the 64 shaft
# vertices behind them. So the swept capsule is the head and nothing else -
# no guesswork, and the shaft does not get to hit people.
#
# THE SPEEDS ARE THE MODEL'S OWN. Posing every sequence and tracking that
# head point through the arm rig gives, in units per second at the head:
#
#     idle1 / idle2 / idle3      peak    3 / 3 / 2      carried
#     holster                    peak   89              carried
#     draw                       peak  133              carried
#     attack1 (= ATTACK1HIT)     peak  287              swung
#     attack2hit                 peak  456              swung
#     attack3 / attack3hit       peak  588              swung
#
# (attack1miss peaks at 837 on its last frame alone, where the animation
# snaps back to idle. That is an animation artefact, not a swing, and it is
# discounted.)
#
# `from 150` is just above the fastest motion on this model that is NOT an
# attack - the draw, at 133. Below that the crowbar is being carried, which
# is the floor's whole job: a player walking about with it should not be
# dealing a trickle of damage to everything they brush past.
#
# EXCEPT THAT THE FLOOR CANNOT DO THAT JOB YET, AND THE NUMBERS ABOVE CANNOT
# SETTLE IT. Every speed in the table is ANIMATION-LOCAL: the arm rig posed in
# place, the viewmodel's own frame. VRStrike_Step is handed a WORLD position
# (vr_strike.h:107) and the plan has the server sweeping the reported pose
# (PCVR_WEAPONS_PLAN.md:488), so in the world frame the head is also carried at
# the player's locomotion speed - and Half-Life's sv_maxspeed is 320 u/s. A
# running player with a perfectly still hand clears both this floor and the
# 300 cap, so walking past something would deal FULL damage rather than none,
# which is the exact opposite of what the floor is for. Worse, VRSTRIKE_REARM
# is 25 u/s, so a moving player's sweep would never re-arm after the first hit.
#
# This is generic to every melee card - the default 60, the knife's 35 and the
# wrench's 90 are all worse - and it cannot be measured out of a card, because
# the fix is to sweep the head's velocity RELATIVE TO THE PLAYER rather than in
# the world. Nothing outside vr_strike_test.cpp calls VRStrike_Step yet, so
# nothing has been wrong in play; the numbers here are honest about the
# animation and will need revisiting the moment melee is wired to a world pose.
#
# `full 300` is the gentlest swing the model performs, attack1 at 287. It
# belongs at the gentlest and not at the average, because in stock Half-Life
# all three crowbar animations do the same `plrDmgCrowbar` (crowbar.cpp:264)
# - the animation chosen is a rotation, not a damage tier. So anything the
# game itself calls a swing is a full hit, and the cap above that is doing
# its other job: a swing amplified by a tracking spike is still one crowbar.
#
# The band is narrow - 150 to 300. The knife's 35-180 and the wrench's 90-420
# are NOT comparable bands and were quoted here as though they were: both of
# those cards put their strike point at 0.00 0.00 0.00, the body bone's origin,
# while this one is 18.95 units out at the head. Re-measured at their own
# declared points, the wrench's fastest swing is 171.7 u/s - so its cap of 420
# is unreachable and it can never score full damage - and the knife's is 435.9.
# A number measured at a different point on a different weapon cannot calibrate
# this one, and the siblings were set by feel in any case.
#
# It is narrow because the gap this model measures between carried and swung is
# narrow, and widening it either way would mean lying:
# downwards, damaging what you walk past; upwards, refusing full damage to a
# blow Half-Life scores at full.
#
# (vr_card_check prints the radius back as 2.89. The card's decimal reader
# is integer arithmetic and truncates, so 2.90 is held as 2.89996 - still
# wide enough to hold the head's farthest vertex at 2.895.)
strike point 5.45 1.61 18.08  radius 2.90  from 150  full 300

# THE PRY TIP IS THE FLATTENED WEDGE, and the mesh says which end that is.
# The bar is 1.28 x 1.28 through the shaft and stays round through the bend,
# but the eight vertices past x 8.0 spread 1.91 units in y and only 0.11 in
# z: a flat plate, which is the prying end and the nail fork. Their centroid
# is this point. The radius is half that measured 1.91-unit width - the width
# of crack the wedge engages - and not half its thickness, because 0.05 is
# not a radius, it is a rounding error.
#
# `from` and `full` are ONE PAIR OF FIELDS shared by strike and pry
# (vr_card.cpp:1006-1014 writes `card->strike.min_speed` either way), so this
# line deliberately carries neither. Repeating them here would silently
# retune the swing, and prying is a torque held against a breakable, not a
# speed.
pry    point 8.14 1.92 18.16  radius 0.95
